00:36:21,830 --> 00:36:26,710
So, can you walk us through the write going through all those layers of the tree?

403
00:36:26,720 --> 00:36:29,630
Absolutely.

404
00:36:29,630 --> 00:36:33,710
So, say you are an engine, you are trying to do an DML operation or trying to do an insert, right?

405
00:36:33,710 --> 00:36:39,830
You generally you can define what size of a file you would like to populate in the storage.

406
00:36:39,830 --> 00:36:43,310
Let's say you want to maintain 256 GB of files, right?

407
00:36:43,310 --> 00:36:46,870
And you want to write 2560.

408
00:36:46,870 --> 00:36:48,870
So, it will create 10 files for you.

409
00:36:48,870 --> 00:36:53,510
And the way it does it, it starts from bottom up.

410
00:36:53,510 --> 00:36:58,790
So, first it will write the files in your storage, object storage.

411
00:36:58,790 --> 00:37:03,550
But other engines querying wouldn't know because it hasn't written that snapshot or that metadata yet.

412
00:37:03,550 --> 00:37:08,790
As soon as it writes it, it will write the manifest file.

413
00:37:08,790 --> 00:37:12,990
In manifest file, then it will populate, okay, these are the files I populated.

414
00:37:12,990 --> 00:37:16,550
These are the min and the max values per column, right?

415
00:37:16,550 --> 00:37:18,270
And other commit time and those kind of things.

416
00:37:18,280 --> 00:37:23,510
And then once it creates that manifest file, it will create a manifest list.

417
00:37:23,520 --> 00:37:30,590
And that manifest list will be like, okay, this is the manifest file I need to refer.

418
00:37:30,590 --> 00:37:33,750
And this is part of X snapshot.

419
00:37:33,750 --> 00:37:37,230
And then it will populate the partition information, say, right? If you have any partition information.

420
00:37:37,240 --> 00:37:39,470
It'll populate that there.

421
00:37:39,470 --> 00:37:46,630
Once it's done with all of this, it will try to write a very final thing, the metadata JSON.

422
00:37:46,630 --> 00:37:49,190
And that's where it does compare and swap.

423
00:37:49,190 --> 00:37:56,830
It starts with the latest version and then compares and So, what it does is it tries to figure out, okay, I started writing those 10 files,

424
00:37:56,830 --> 00:38:02,350
but in the meantime, did the metadata JSON change?

425
00:38:02,350 --> 00:38:12,750
If it changed, it has to redo the newer version of the JSON, which otherwise would conflict, or otherwise it will just do version plus one.json and write that metadata JSON file.

426
00:38:12,750 --> 00:38:14,950
And that will become the current file.

427
00:38:14,950 --> 00:38:19,190
Going on forward, there will be read isolation.

428
00:38:19,190 --> 00:38:22,870
Anything that is coming into read it will start reading from that particular commit.

429
00:38:22,880 --> 00:38:26,430
We want our rights to be atomic.

430
00:38:26,430 --> 00:38:29,670
Now, this process you described there are multiple files that need to be changed.

431
00:38:29,680 --> 00:38:33,630
Something could fail before it's completed the process.

432
00:38:33,640 --> 00:38:46,910
Does it depend on the final step being created and that the system can tolerate, let's say, dangling references or files with nothing pointing to them from prior failures?

433
00:38:46,920 --> 00:38:51,670
It solves all the problems that you talked about. They They are seeing the problem with the hive as well, right?

434
00:38:51,680 --> 00:38:53,670
So, there are two kind of thing.

435
00:38:53,670 --> 00:38:55,790
One is read isolation, one is write.

436
00:38:55,790 --> 00:39:05,430
In terms of reads, what it does is like for the audience, if you start at 2:00 p.m. reading something and at 2:01 p.m. something changed,

437
00:39:05,430 --> 00:39:15,190
but they give you results at 2:05, you still see the result at 2:00 p.m. It locked you there at the very first time you started reading.

438
00:39:15,190 --> 00:39:19,750
But for the rights, the way it does it, it's optimistic write isolation.

439
00:39:19,750 --> 00:39:24,990
To put it simply, say Robert is trying to write and Sahil is trying to write.

440
00:39:24,990 --> 00:39:28,550
Whoever first writes get the JSON, the other person will get the rejection.

441
00:39:28,550 --> 00:39:33,310
And it will go to that engine, okay, Sahil, Robert committed, go back and start writing the engines.

442
00:39:33,320 --> 00:39:38,630
But what it doesn't have to do is to rewrite the file.

443
00:39:38,630 --> 00:39:40,871
It only has to write the metadata. The files stay the same.

444
00:39:40,880 --> 00:39:46,310
But assume something happens, the world crashes and everything and say it's all of the operations leave in between.

445
00:39:46,310 --> 00:39:53,190
You have those orphan files, but they are not rendered anywhere because there's no reference in the metadata.

446
00:39:53,190 --> 00:39:55,870
So, they won't give you corrupt reads.

447
00:39:55,870 --> 00:40:00,390
The only thing you will have to do is clean up your storage.

448
00:40:00,390 --> 00:40:05,510
And that operation has to be done to remove the orphan files if you identify. But those are very rare scenarios.

449
00:40:05,520 --> 00:40:12,390
Does Iceberg support anything for managing the size of data either I

450
00:40:12,390 --> 00:40:18,390
no longer need historical time travel beyond a certain date in the past or

451
00:40:18,390 --> 00:40:25,830
I can de-res the data by going from every single change to once a week or

452
00:40:25,840 --> 00:40:32,910
something like Postgres vacuum that is just cleaning up spaces created by write operations.

453
00:40:32,920 --> 00:40:35,670
Yeah, there's something called snapshot expiry.

454
00:40:35,680 --> 00:40:45,990
So, let's say you're doing a lot of DML operation every 15 minutes and you don't necessarily need snapshot from a week ago or week ago later, right?

455
00:40:45,990 --> 00:40:54,510
What you do is you can configure through engines that I want to expire or remove the snapshots after a certain date.

456
00:40:54,510 --> 00:40:59,430
And that's where you will see, okay, you can't go back beyond a certain date.

457
00:40:59,430 --> 00:41:05,230
And that's a decisions team make depending on their workloads, what they want to do, those kind of things, right?

458
00:41:05,230 --> 00:41:12,790
And similarly for the I think you talked about the vacuum, there's something called vacuum full in Postgres, if I'm not wrong.

459
00:41:12,790 --> 00:41:17,750
So, essentially what it means is say you have committed small files over a period of time.

460
00:41:17,750 --> 00:41:21,830
And over the weekend, you get smaller chunk of files, for example.

461
00:41:21,830 --> 00:41:23,390
But over the weekend, you get a proper trend.

462
00:41:23,390 --> 00:41:32,270
But what you want to do is you want to make sure your whole bucket is clean and you don't want to maintain small files because it still has to read it, still has to do everything.

463
00:41:32,270 --> 00:41:41,350
What you can do is you can merge all small files into a standard large files, which is best for your read patterns and it's called data compaction.

464
00:41:41,350 --> 00:41:46,590
And a lot of engines provide that kind of service when you do manage service with them, but you can manage by yourself as well.

465
00:41:46,600 --> 00:41:56,310
Most databases have something like the query planner that decides what order to access different tables in what in a join.

466
00:41:56,320 --> 00:42:08,790
We haven't talked about joins, but does Iceberg support joins and does it have a query planner or is that process you described where it's walking through the metadata to find what it needs, is that the query planner?

467
00:42:08,800 --> 00:42:14,390
Yeah, so Iceberg handles all of those things to an engine.

468
00:42:14,390 --> 00:42:24,470
What it does is it tells you, "Okay, this is the metadata I have and this is the metadata you should pass through to do the query fetch."

469
00:42:24,470 --> 00:42:29,431
But, it doesn't tell you, "This is how you should do it." It tells you the spec.

470
00:42:29,431 --> 00:42:31,070
It tells you that this is what I have.

471
00:42:31,070 --> 00:42:38,910
This is how you can traverse through the tree of the metadata, but it doesn't tell you, "Okay, this is up to the engines to implement."

472
00:42:38,920 --> 00:42:46,471
So far, we've been talking about a single table or we haven't been specific. Does Iceberg support joins?

473
00:42:46,480 --> 00:42:48,870
Yes, Iceberg does support joins.

474
00:42:48,870 --> 00:42:54,030
You can connect multiple tables together to have a rendered results as you need.

475
00:42:54,030 --> 00:43:04,790
You can join Iceberg to Iceberg or in case of several engines, let's say have their custom table format, you can connect Iceberg to a custom table format, but that's something engine does. But, to answer your question, yes.

476
00:43:04,800 --> 00:43:09,670
So, my next question is going to be is there a query planner?

477
00:43:09,680 --> 00:43:17,270
But, since Iceberg is not an engine, it's just a format, what's the right question I should be asking?

478
00:43:17,280 --> 00:43:21,310
So, to first we answer the question that it does support join, yes.

479
00:43:21,310 --> 00:43:25,670
And to a follow-up question, it's very much engine dependent.

480
00:43:25,670 --> 00:43:30,710
Iceberg does provide the stats for better join planning, right? But, it doesn't execute the joins.

481
00:43:30,720 --> 00:43:39,430
Your engine has to plan those joins architecturally the query plan per se based on that, but Iceberg doesn't prescribe that.

482
00:43:39,440 --> 00:43:44,750
Are there multiple engines in the marketplace from different vendors?

483
00:43:44,750 --> 00:43:47,911
Are they commercial? Are they open source?

484
00:43:47,920 --> 00:43:51,790
Yeah, there are multiple engine options you have.

485
00:43:51,790 --> 00:43:55,830
You have proprietary ones, the commercial ones, and you have the open ones.

486
00:43:55,830 --> 00:43:57,550
You can use Spark, the Flink, the Trino, which is open source.

487
00:43:57,550 --> 00:44:07,070
But, you can use Snowflake, you can use Databricks, you can use BigQuery, anything which is compatible to the Iceberg spec can use it.

488
00:44:07,080 --> 00:44:11,510
You also mentioned there are multiple options for catalog.

489
00:44:11,510 --> 00:44:20,110
As I was reading about this, I found this list consisting of Hadoop, Hive, AWS Glue, and some others.

490
00:44:20,110 --> 00:44:22,310
What are some of the more popular catalogs?

491
00:44:22,320 --> 00:44:25,670
Sure.

492
00:44:25,670 --> 00:44:32,910
AWS Glue is one of them, and AWS Glue recently became compatible with IRC, which is Iceberg rest compatible, right?

493
00:44:32,910 --> 00:44:38,550
And then you have Snowflake's Horizon, Databricks Unity Catalog, and Hive Metastore.

494
00:44:38,550 --> 00:44:41,790
But Hive Metastore is usually I haven't seen being used a lot,

495
00:44:41,800 --> 00:44:51,190
just because with cloud at a Hive Metastore was built better for the HDFS file and everything, but it's it's one of the options as well.

496
00:44:51,200 --> 00:44:56,110
What are some differentiators between different catalogs?

497
00:44:56,120 --> 00:44:58,910
Very good question.

498
00:44:58,910 --> 00:45:00,750
So, it really varies what you are trying to do.

499
00:45:00,750 --> 00:45:02,830
So, let me put it this way.

500
00:45:02,830 --> 00:45:07,430
As we talked about Iceberg spec, there's several versions of it.

501
00:45:07,430 --> 00:45:09,590
Version one, version two, version three.

502
00:45:09,590 --> 00:45:14,870
So, as an example, the current version that is GA is version three, which supports nanoseconds time support.

503
00:45:14,880 --> 00:45:18,790
So, not all catalogs would support it.

504
00:45:18,790 --> 00:45:27,030
So, you have to think through how the implementations are, what you're looking into, say Spark is writing it, Snowflake is reading it.

505
00:45:27,040 --> 00:45:29,070
Can that catalog support it, right?

506
00:45:29,070 --> 00:45:33,830
And then another thing you want to see is how the RBAX are handled.

507
00:45:33,830 --> 00:45:41,310
So, say since this is interoperable, you want to make sure that your governance gets applied from one side to another, right?

508
00:45:41,310 --> 00:45:47,230
So, say you have access via Spark, but you don't have access via Snowflake to a certain area.

509
00:45:47,230 --> 00:45:50,950
You want to make sure you have the right roles, and the governance is handled via the catalog.

510
00:45:50,950 --> 00:45:57,390
How strong are they with the governance, how they vent the credentials to the storage, those aspects.

511
00:45:57,390 --> 00:46:00,350
So, it's a variety, and I would evaluate in a way wherein what's my use case.

512
00:46:00,360 --> 00:46:11,230
I I to move now for the last part of the interview talking about the bigger picture of Iceberg in the application architecture for an entire application.

513
00:46:11,240 --> 00:46:17,190
The idea of a CRUD application is extremely widespread.

514
00:46:17,190 --> 00:46:23,910
Almost everything we use on the internet for business is some kind of CRUD application that writes into OLTP.

515
00:46:23,910 --> 00:46:30,070
By application, I mean something has a database, a middle tier, and a user interface.

516
00:46:30,070 --> 00:46:35,990
What would be uh some examples of a database application where Iceberg is the data layer?

517
00:46:35,990 --> 00:46:39,390
What is that application doing? What is its architecture?

518
00:46:39,400 --> 00:46:44,550
I can give one of my examples that I recently implemented.

519
00:46:44,550 --> 00:46:50,711
We had clickstream data coming in, and the clickstream data landed in Postgres. Postgres is OLTP.

520
00:46:50,720 --> 00:46:55,630
And then, from there, it landed via the Pulsar.

521
00:46:55,630 --> 00:47:01,190
Pulsar Apache Pulsar is again for streaming as well as uh managing the queues.

522
00:47:01,190 --> 00:47:06,230
What it did is it holds data for about 15 minutes, and then pushes to Snowflake.

523
00:47:06,230 --> 00:47:14,910
And then, when it lands to Snowflake, that's where you will do a lot of your cleaning up, transformations you want to do, and you prepare it or curate it for other teams.

524
00:47:14,910 --> 00:47:21,630
And then, ultimately, the teams wanted to implement the ML work or the model on the Spark side.

525
00:47:21,630 --> 00:47:26,030
And that's where the interoperability come in.

526
00:47:26,030 --> 00:47:30,630
They were able to use the Snowflake engine to do a lot of my transformations and loading for the right tables.

527
00:47:30,630 --> 00:47:40,870
And but the ML teams could leverage the Spark engine for the ML workloads to buy just accessing the table that were written by Snowflake on S3.

528
00:47:40,870 --> 00:47:48,590
And to summarize this, I would put it in a way wherein your multiple teams need access to the same data,

529
00:47:48,600 --> 00:47:54,750
but you don't want to don't want to have a redundant storage in terms of just okay for the same use case.

530
00:47:54,750 --> 00:47:56,550
You don't want to have that.

531
00:47:56,550 --> 00:47:58,910
It's better you don't do it.

532
00:47:58,910 --> 00:48:05,950
Secondly, I have seen a lot of teams do doing the similar transformation or almost similar transformation just to give different kind of data to different teams, right?

533
00:48:05,960 --> 00:48:08,430
It reduces that.

534
00:48:08,430 --> 00:48:16,150
Another thing is, if you have use cases wherein you want to do anything analytical, that's where I will use it.

535
00:48:16,150 --> 00:48:24,470
Again, AI is one of the use cases that's uh getting very much popular in which what happens is you can have Git-like branching and

536
00:48:24,470 --> 00:48:32,030
you can do AB testing on the data with Nessie catalog and you do branch the data into two layers and do analysis on both layers.

537
00:48:32,040 --> 00:48:40,950
By AI being an application, are we talking more about training or fine-tuning or

538
00:48:40,950 --> 00:48:49,390
about using the data to drive inference of models you already have in order to evaluate the models or what do we mean by AI?

539
00:48:49,400 --> 00:48:51,230
Okay, I just the training the data.

540
00:48:51,230 --> 00:49:03,070
If you're training the data with data set A versus B and you have a branch-like structure, data branch, not a Git branch, data branch-like structure, you can have A and B comparison while training and do analysis.

541
00:49:03,080 --> 00:49:08,830
Well, see here we've covered Iceberg from beginning to end.

542
00:49:08,830 --> 00:49:13,510
Before we wrap up, is anything else about Iceberg that you'd like listeners to take away?

543
00:49:13,520 --> 00:49:20,470
What I would say is a very exciting field and it's a very exciting world of the Iceberg.

544
00:49:20,470 --> 00:49:26,430
Now, Iceberg team is working and collaborating on the version V4.

545
00:49:26,430 --> 00:49:28,870
It's not final yet, but it's going towards even better product.

546
00:49:28,870 --> 00:49:37,110
And another thing that I've been noticing in industry is that vendors are converging to Iceberg as a standard.

547
00:49:37,110 --> 00:49:41,870
They are now not limiting themselves to their own proprietary one.

548
00:49:41,870 --> 00:49:43,750
So, I think it's a good time to adopt.

549
00:49:43,750 --> 00:49:50,790
Another thing that I would recommend teams to take away is be more involved in Apache's dev list uh for the Iceberg.

550
00:49:50,790 --> 00:49:58,710
You'll get to know how it's developing, what the discussion themes are, what the common themes are, and you'll get to know how things are developing in the world of iceberg.

551
00:49:58,710 --> 00:50:01,830
It's a truly, truly exciting time to be a learning iceberg.

